From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Philippe Simons <simons.philippe@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 01:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] net: qrtr: send the pending HELLO instead of dropping an early
 packet

v7.3 moved the HELLO handshake from the name service into the core
("net: qrtr: Send HELLO message on endpoint register", Chris Lew): each
node sends HELLO from delayed work, and qrtr_node_enqueue() drops every
non-HELLO packet with -EAGAIN until that HELLO has gone out.

That opens a race at remoteproc boot. The remote's HELLO can reach the
name service before our HELLO work has run; ctrl_cmd_hello() answers
with announce_servers(), every NEW_SERVER is dropped ("failed to
announce new service"), and nothing ever re-sends them. Services that
registered before the remote came up stay invisible to it.

HW (MANGMI Air X, SM6115): rmtfs and tqftpserv register at 16.3s, the
modem boots at 16.6s and loses the announcement, its watchdog fires at
56.6s ("DOG detects stalled initialization"), and only the recovery
boot brings up WCN3990 Wi-Fi -- or none, on a boot where recovery does
not win the race either. Fine on 7.2.11, which sent HELLO from the name
service before answering.

When a packet would be dropped only because our HELLO is still queued,
flush the HELLO work and re-check. Every caller runs in process context
(the name service worker, sendmsg, recvmsg's resume-tx) and the HELLO
work takes neither qrtr_node_lock nor anything else those hold.
---
--- a/net/qrtr/af_qrtr.c
+++ b/net/qrtr/af_qrtr.c
@@ -347,15 +347,33 @@
 {
 	struct qrtr_hdr_v1 *hdr;
 	size_t len = skb->len;
+	bool hello_sent;
 	int rc, confirm_rx;
 
 	mutex_lock(&node->ep_lock);
-	if (!node->hello_sent && type != QRTR_TYPE_HELLO) {
+	hello_sent = node->hello_sent;
+	mutex_unlock(&node->ep_lock);
+
+	if (!hello_sent && type != QRTR_TYPE_HELLO) {
+		/*
+		 * The remote can say HELLO before our own HELLO work has run,
+		 * and the name service answers it at once with the servers it
+		 * knows. Dropping that answer loses it for good: the remote
+		 * never learns of services registered before it came up (rmtfs,
+		 * tftp), stalls, and the modem watchdog resets it 40s later.
+		 * Send the pending HELLO now instead, then re-check.
+		 */
+		flush_delayed_work(&node->say_hello);
+
+		mutex_lock(&node->ep_lock);
+		hello_sent = node->hello_sent;
 		mutex_unlock(&node->ep_lock);
-		kfree_skb(skb);
-		return -EAGAIN;
+
+		if (!hello_sent) {
+			kfree_skb(skb);
+			return -EAGAIN;
+		}
 	}
-	mutex_unlock(&node->ep_lock);
 
 	confirm_rx = qrtr_tx_wait(node, to->sq_node, to->sq_port, type);
 	if (confirm_rx < 0) {
